1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,827
Welcome to StockPilot, a multi warehouse inventory and procurement platform, built as an original
DevOps capstone.

2
00:00:07,827 --> 00:00:15,654
The product follows a physical item from a supplier purchase through receiving, reservation,
transfer and fulfilment.

3
00:00:15,654 --> 00:00:24,459
Around that product is a delivery platform using containers, Kubernetes, automated security gates,
monitoring and infrastructure as code.

4
00:00:24,459 --> 00:00:29,351
This presentation explains the implemented design and the available verification.

5
00:00:29,351 --> 00:00:34,732
The diagrams are original illustrations, not screenshots of a live cluster.

6
00:00:34,732 --> 00:00:37,667
The audio is synthetic English narration.

7
00:00:38,667 --> 00:00:44,521
Inventory management is a demanding domain because several workflows compete for the same stock.

8
00:00:44,521 --> 00:00:47,866
A sales reservation promises units to a customer.

9
00:00:47,866 --> 00:00:50,794
A transfer moves those units between warehouses.

10
00:00:50,794 --> 00:00:54,557
A purchase order brings more units into the business.

11
00:00:54,557 --> 00:00:57,066
A simple counter is not enough.

12
00:00:57,066 --> 00:01:04,593
StockPilot keeps the available quantity separate from the physical quantity, makes transfers atomic,
and records stock movement history.

13
00:01:04,593 --> 00:01:12,539
The architecture is designed to support multiple replicas, but no production capacity or load test
result is claimed here.

14
00:01:13,539 --> 00:01:17,191
Stock Pilot combines several stacks with specific responsibilities.

15
00:01:17,191 --> 00:01:20,387
React and Type Script provide the interface.

16
00:01:20,387 --> 00:01:26,322
Fast A P I, SQL Alchemy and Alembic implement validated transactions and migrations.

17
00:01:26,322 --> 00:01:28,148
Postgres owns durable inventory.

18
00:01:28,148 --> 00:01:30,887
Redis and Celery handle asynchronous reports.

19
00:01:30,887 --> 00:01:39,105
Docker and Helm package the runtime, while GitHub Actions, Argo C D and Terraform define delivery
and infrastructure.

20
00:01:40,105 --> 00:01:47,825
The browser reaches the A P I through a proxy or ingress, and the A P I reads and writes Postgres.

21
00:01:47,825 --> 00:01:51,134
Report requests and the scheduler enqueue work in Redis.

22
00:01:51,134 --> 00:01:55,545
Celery workers read the database and write timestamped reports into the cache.

23
00:01:55,545 --> 00:01:58,853
Workers and A P I replicas have separate responsibilities.

24
00:01:58,853 --> 00:02:00,691
Redis never owns authoritative stock.

25
00:02:01,691 --> 00:02:04,916
Suppliers connect to products and purchase orders.

26
00:02:04,916 --> 00:02:09,062
Each balance belongs to one product and one warehouse.

27
00:02:09,062 --> 00:02:15,050
Database constraints require nonnegative stock and prevent reserved units from exceeding physical
stock.

28
00:02:15,050 --> 00:02:17,814
Movements record receipts, issues and transfers.

29
00:02:17,814 --> 00:02:19,196
Reservations record commitments.

30
00:02:19,196 --> 00:02:26,106
Foreign keys preserve the meaning of inventory history when catalogue records are changed or
removed.

31
00:02:27,106 --> 00:02:34,207
Eight views organize the frontend: overview, inventory, movements, reservations, purchase orders,
warehouses, suppliers and reports.

32
00:02:34,207 --> 00:02:41,781
Users can search products, export C S V data, manage catalogue records and perform stock workflows.

33
00:02:41,781 --> 00:02:44,621
Forms include validation and recoverable errors.

34
00:02:44,621 --> 00:02:48,408
Thirteen frontend tests exercise components and request payloads.

35
00:02:48,408 --> 00:02:53,142
This feature map is a diagram, not a browser screenshot.

36
00:02:54,142 --> 00:02:58,726
In this illustrative transfer, twenty units move from Delhi to Bengaluru.

37
00:02:58,726 --> 00:03:04,560
Delhi falls from one hundred to eighty, while Bengaluru rises from forty to sixty.

38
00:03:04,560 --> 00:03:07,477
The total remains one hundred and forty.

39
00:03:07,477 --> 00:03:10,394
Both balances and the movement commit together.

40
00:03:10,394 --> 00:03:12,061
Available stock excludes reservations.

41
00:03:12,061 --> 00:03:17,479
An identical idempotent retry returns the existing movement instead of moving stock again.

42
00:03:18,479 --> 00:03:21,881
Available stock equals on hand minus reserved.

43
00:03:21,881 --> 00:03:24,312
Releasing a reservation restores availability.

44
00:03:24,312 --> 00:03:27,228
Fulfilling creates the matching issue transaction.

45
00:03:27,228 --> 00:03:34,034
The same completion can be retried safely, while conflicting changes after closure are rejected.

46
00:03:34,034 --> 00:03:37,923
This preserves both physical stock and customer promises.

47
00:03:38,923 --> 00:03:43,129
Purchase orders move from draft to ordered to received.

48
00:03:43,129 --> 00:03:45,933
Lines must match the selected supplier.

49
00:03:45,933 --> 00:03:51,542
Receiving commits all receipt movements together, with deterministic keys preventing duplicate
stock.

50
00:03:51,542 --> 00:03:52,944
Drafts are editable.

51
00:03:52,944 --> 00:03:56,683
Partial deliveries and invoice matching remain future work.

52
00:03:57,683 --> 00:04:01,446
The scheduler or report endpoint queues work in Redis.

53
00:04:01,446 --> 00:04:06,882
A Celery worker reads Postgres and caches a timestamped report for three minutes.

54
00:04:06,882 --> 00:04:13,991
The live dashboard uses its own database request, so report cache failures do not change
authoritative inventory.

55
00:04:14,991 --> 00:04:19,531
Postgres row locks coordinate competing stock changes across replicas.

56
00:04:19,531 --> 00:04:25,081
Stable product ordering reduces deadlock risk, while request fingerprints protect retries.

57
00:04:25,081 --> 00:04:28,613
SQLite tests verify rules, not production locking.

58
00:04:28,613 --> 00:04:35,171
Concurrent Postgres tests and measured load tests are still required before capacity claims.

59
00:04:36,171 --> 00:04:43,937
The delivery workflow connects tests and security gates to image builds, scans and a temporary kind
deployment.

60
00:04:43,937 --> 00:04:47,591
Commit S H A tags make releases traceable.

61
00:04:47,591 --> 00:04:51,246
Promotion updates Git only after its prerequisites pass.

62
00:04:51,246 --> 00:04:54,900
Hosted execution and registry publication require separate evidence.

63
00:04:55,900 --> 00:05:01,985
Kubernetes defines replicas, Services, Ingress, probes, storage and one migration job.

64
00:05:01,985 --> 00:05:06,963
Autoscaling, disruption budgets and resource limits express operational expectations.

65
00:05:06,963 --> 00:05:09,176
Readiness checks database access.

66
00:05:09,176 --> 00:05:17,473
A rendered chart validates configuration; actual rollout, persistence and scaling still need a
running cluster.

67
00:05:18,473 --> 00:05:22,757
Release checks cover source, dependencies, secrets, infrastructure and images.

68
00:05:22,757 --> 00:05:26,089
Runtime controls reduce privileges and restrict access.

69
00:05:26,089 --> 00:05:34,180
The product currently has no sign in or role based permissions, so demo write endpoints remain
private.

70
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:37,512
Scan results apply to their specific revision.

71
00:05:38,512 --> 00:05:41,998
Prometheus records request counts, errors and latency.

72
00:05:41,998 --> 00:05:47,974
Grafana panels and alerts organize those metrics, while structured logs support investigation.

73
00:05:47,974 --> 00:05:52,954
Argo C D reconciles the desired Helm release from Git.

74
00:05:52,954 --> 00:05:57,935
Live graphs and healthy synchronized status require real runtime evidence.

75
00:05:58,935 --> 00:06:03,457
Terraform defines networking, E K S workers and storage.

76
00:06:03,457 --> 00:06:06,975
NAT is optional, with its tradeoffs documented.

77
00:06:06,975 --> 00:06:10,995
No cloud deployment is claimed without account access.

78
00:06:10,995 --> 00:06:18,533
Planning, applying, verification and teardown are separate milestones, and retained data requires
deliberate cleanup decisions.

79
00:06:19,533 --> 00:06:22,764
The evidence register separates implementation from execution.

80
00:06:22,764 --> 00:06:25,995
Local tests and builds have captured results.

81
00:06:25,995 --> 00:06:34,765
Docker and Chromium are blocked in this environment, so this video shows architecture rather than a
simulated live application.

82
00:06:34,765 --> 00:06:37,997
Hosted delivery and cloud deployment remain unclaimed.

83
00:06:38,997 --> 00:06:49,270
The live runbook starts with stock receiving, transfers, reservations and purchase orders, then
demonstrates delivery, persistence, monitoring and GitOps.

84
00:06:49,270 --> 00:06:52,514
Capture each result only after execution.

85
00:06:52,514 --> 00:06:59,543
OpsPulse informed the documentation organization; Stock Pilot uses original implementation, diagrams
and narration.

86
00:06:59,543 --> 00:07:00,624
Thank you.
